{#
  Migrations 0071 / 0071a / 0071b — THE COMPLETION RECORD AND THE EPOCH PIN.
  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5 (verbatim), §4.1 law 3, §16 MI07/MI09;
  spec/TRAPPOINT-SPEC.md §5.4, spec/invariants/I03-epoch-pin.md, I04;
  merge-gate-invariant.md §3.3 and anomalies A2, A3, A4;
  kernel plan ruling D1; conformance cases CF-09, CF-40, CF-44.

  ── THE ANOMALY THIS ANSWERS IS NOT A SERIALIZABILITY BUG ────────────────────────
  A precursor discovered at T+e and inserted against a permit merged at T is a PERFECTLY
  SERIALIZABLE history. There is no read-write dependency to detect, no anomaly for the
  concurrency control to refuse; SERIALIZABLE orders writes, it does not prevent late
  arrival (§4.1 law 3). Every competitor treats this as an alerting problem. It is a
  referential-integrity problem.

  Once a row here references `(subject_id, e)` through a composite foreign key declared
  ON UPDATE RESTRICT, CockroachDB refuses ANY update that changes the subject's
  `gate_epoch` — and every new obligation must change it, because materialising an
  obligation increments the epoch in the same statement that increments the counter. So
  attaching a new precursor to an issued subject is not forbidden, it is IMPOSSIBLE: the
  write fails on referential integrity before any policy, any trigger and any role is
  consulted. The writer is forced onto the declared path — suspend the issued subject,
  fork a child whose gate is cleared afresh. That is git discipline (never rewrite a
  merged commit; open a revert branch) expressed as a foreign key.

  ── NEVER CASCADE, IN EITHER POSITION ────────────────────────────────────────────
  `ON UPDATE RESTRICT ON DELETE RESTRICT`, stated explicitly on both statements. A
  cascade rewrites history, which is the precise offence this substrate exists to detect:
  ON UPDATE CASCADE would carry the pin forward to the new epoch and the gate would
  silently reopen; ON DELETE CASCADE would let deleting a permit erase the proof that it
  was ever issued. Neither is a default anyone should have to remember, so neither is
  left to a default.

  ── RULING D1 — THE "PARTIAL-FK PAIR" IS MATCH SIMPLE, SPELLED OUT ───────────────
  §5.5 writes `FOREIGN KEY (subject_id, gate_epoch)` and then remarks that the two kinds
  are "enforced by a partial-FK pair, one per kind". CockroachDB has no conditional
  foreign key and never will; what it has is the SQL standard's MATCH SIMPLE semantics,
  under which a composite foreign key containing ANY NULL column is NOT ENFORCED. That is
  exactly, and only, the "one per kind" behaviour the architecture describes.

  So the foreign-key columns are `permit_id` and `cr_id`, each nullable, and each carries
  its own composite pin. A permit's merge record leaves `cr_id` NULL, so `epoch_pin_cr`
  does not apply to it; a change request's leaves `permit_id` NULL, so `epoch_pin_permit`
  does not apply to it. MEASURED, not assumed: on cockroachdb/cockroach:v26.2.5, a row
  with `cr_id` NULL is admitted while `epoch_pin_cr` exists, and mutating the pinned epoch
  returns 23503 naming the pin.

  `subject_id` stays NOT NULL and is the second half of the primary key, because the
  completion record must be addressable by (kind, id) without knowing which nullable
  column holds it. Three CHECKs bind the polymorphism so the two representations can never
  disagree: exactly one reference is present, `subject_id` equals whichever one it is, and
  the kind token agrees with which one it is. Without the second CHECK, a row could pin
  permit P's epoch while claiming to complete permit Q, and the pin would be enforced
  against the wrong subject while every constraint still looked satisfied.

  ── ONE COMPLETION PER SUBJECT, AND THE PK NAME IS AN EXHIBIT ────────────────────
  `PRIMARY KEY (subject_kind, subject_id)` is declared WITHOUT a CONSTRAINT name on
  purpose. The conformance manifest fixes the exhibit for CF-09 ("merge the same subject
  twice") and CF-44 ("N parallel merges yield exactly one merge record") as
  `merge_record_pkey`, which is the name CockroachDB derives for an unnamed primary key.
  Naming it `pk_merge_record` here would rename the exhibit and both cases would fail on
  the constraint name while passing on the SQLSTATE — the exact failure mode the manifest
  exists to prevent. Verified on v26.2.5: an unnamed table-level PRIMARY KEY on
  `merge_record` yields `merge_record_pkey`.

  ── WHAT THIS TEMPLATE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT EMIT ────────────────────────────────
  §5.5 writes `merged_commit BYTES NOT NULL REFERENCES mainline.commit_obj (commit_id)`.
  `commit_obj` is VERTICAL (MAINLINE migration 0024, an authored band) and `merge_record`
  is SUBSTRATE (MR-2 names it), so a substrate template may not name it: the rendered file
  would fail against the reference binding with 42P01, outside the refusal taxonomy. The
  column keeps NOT NULL and the 32-byte width CHECK that says it is a content address
  rather than a counter; a vertical owning a commit relation adds the foreign key in its
  own authored band.
#}
{% if subjects | length != 2 %}
{# The completion record's shape is fixed by the migration numbering: 0071a and 0071b are
   the two pins §18 allocates, and the exactly-one CHECK below is written as the
   architecture writes it, `(a IS NULL) <> (b IS NULL)`, which is a two-term form. A
   binding with a different number of gated subject kinds needs a numbering decision and
   a cardinality expression that this template does not get to invent silently. #}
{{ the_kernel_allocates_epoch_pin_migrations_for_exactly_two_subject_kinds }}
{% endif %}
{#
  Per-kind constants the render context does not carry: the pin's constraint name (the
  binding declares `epoch_pin_constraint`, which the context does not yet expose) and the
  migration number, which is a §18 / MR-8 anchor cited by number in the corpus.
#}
{% set pin_spec = {
     'permit':         {'file': '0071a_epoch_pin_permit.sql', 'constraint': 'epoch_pin_permit'},
     'change_request': {'file': '0071b_epoch_pin_cr.sql',     'constraint': 'epoch_pin_cr'},
   } %}
-- @file 0071_merge_record.sql
{{ header(file='0071_merge_record.sql',
          title='CREATE TABLE ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.merge_record — at most one completion per subject, ever',
          rationale='The completion record is what the epoch pin hangs from, and its primary key is what makes a double merge 23505 rather than two rows nobody compares. It is subject-polymorphic because both gated kinds complete into it: one nullable reference per kind plus a composite foreign key per kind, which under MATCH SIMPLE is enforced exactly when its reference is present — the only way this platform can express a conditional foreign key. Three CHECKs bind the polymorphic subject_id to whichever reference is non-null, so a record can never pin one subject while claiming to complete another.',
          mi=['MI07', 'MI09'], i=['I03', 'I04']) }}
--
-- clearance_digest      sha256 over the sorted (obligation_id, disposition_id) set that
--                       cleared this gate. It is the answer to "what exactly was signed
--                       off, at the moment of issue" that does not depend on those rows
--                       still being joinable years later.
-- checkpoint_tree_size  the custody ledger's tree size at issue, NULLABLE on purpose: a
--                       merge that happened while the ledger was unwitnessed is recorded
--                       honestly as unwitnessed rather than given a fabricated size. The
--                       unwitnessed-debt table is where that gap is accounted.
-- merged_by             the acting identity, as a subject claim, not a foreign key: the
--                       person table is versioned by (signer_sub, effective_from) and a
--                       single-column reference would demand a UNIQUE index on
--                       signer_sub, which is false by design.
--
-- NOTE ON ORDERING. This row is written BEFORE the subject reaches its completing state
-- (spec §5.3 steps 5 then 7). DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED is unimplemented on this
-- platform, so every intermediate state must be legal at a statement boundary and THE
-- LAST WRITE MUST BE THE ONE THAT TRIPS. Writing the completion record first is what puts
-- the pin in place before the state change the CHECKs guard.

CREATE TABLE {{ binding.schema }}.merge_record (
  {{ '%-20s'|format('subject_kind') }} STRING NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-20s'|format('subject_id') }} UUID   NOT NULL,
{% for s in subjects %}
  {{ '%-20s'|format(s.id_column) }} UUID   NULL,
{% endfor %}
  {{ '%-20s'|format('gate_epoch') }} INT8   NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-20s'|format('merged_at') }} TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  {{ '%-20s'|format('merged_by') }} STRING NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-20s'|format('merged_commit') }} BYTES  NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-20s'|format('clearance_digest') }} BYTES  NOT NULL,
  {{ '%-20s'|format('checkpoint_tree_size') }} INT8   NULL,

  CONSTRAINT merge_subject_kind_known
    CHECK (subject_kind IN ({% for s in subjects %}'{{ s.kind }}'{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %})),
  -- MATCH SIMPLE made explicit: exactly one reference is present, so exactly one of the
  -- two composite pins is enforced on any given row.
  CONSTRAINT merge_exactly_one_subject
    CHECK (({{ subjects[0].id_column }} IS NULL) <> ({{ subjects[1].id_column }} IS NULL)),
  -- The polymorphic key and the foreign-key columns are the SAME subject. Without this,
  -- a record could pin one subject's epoch while claiming to complete another.
  CONSTRAINT merge_subject_id_bound
    CHECK (subject_id = COALESCE({% for s in subjects %}{{ s.id_column }}{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %})),
  CONSTRAINT merge_subject_matches
    CHECK ({% for s in subjects %}(subject_kind = '{{ s.kind }}') = ({{ s.id_column }} IS NOT NULL){{ '
       AND ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}),
  CONSTRAINT merge_epoch_nonneg           CHECK (gate_epoch >= 0),
  CONSTRAINT merge_merged_by_stated       CHECK (merged_by <> ''),
  CONSTRAINT merge_commit_sized           CHECK (length(merged_commit) = 32),
  CONSTRAINT merge_clearance_digest_sized CHECK (length(clearance_digest) = 32),
  CONSTRAINT merge_checkpoint_nonneg
    CHECK (checkpoint_tree_size IS NULL OR checkpoint_tree_size >= 0),
  -- UNNAMED ON PURPOSE. CockroachDB derives `merge_record_pkey`, which is the exhibit the
  -- conformance manifest fixes for CF-09 and CF-44. Naming it here would rename the
  -- exhibit and both cases would fail on the constraint name while passing on the code.
  PRIMARY KEY (subject_kind, subject_id)
);
{% for s in subjects %}
{% set pin = pin_spec[s.kind] %}
-- @file {{ pin.file }}
{{ header(file=pin.file,
          title='ALTER TABLE ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.merge_record ADD CONSTRAINT ' ~ pin.constraint ~ ' — the pin',
          rationale='Late-arriving recall is not a serializability failure and cannot be answered by an isolation level: a precursor inserted after a merge is a perfectly serializable history. Once this foreign key references (subject_id, epoch), ON UPDATE RESTRICT makes any change to that epoch impossible, and every new obligation must change it — so attaching a precursor to a completed subject fails on referential integrity before any policy is consulted. CASCADE appears in neither position because a cascade rewrites history, which is the offence this substrate exists to detect.',
          mi=['MI07'], i=['I03']) }}
--
-- Declared as an ALTER rather than inline with the table, because one statement per file
-- is not negotiable and because the two pins reference two tables that are created in two
-- earlier migrations; a single CREATE TABLE naming both would be a file whose failure
-- mode nobody can diagnose.
--
-- Under MATCH SIMPLE this constraint is enforced on exactly those rows whose
-- `{{ s.id_column }}` is non-null, which is exactly the rows completing a
-- {{ s.kind }}. A row for the other kind leaves the column NULL and the constraint does
-- not apply to it. That is the "partial-FK pair, one per kind" of §5.5, spelled out.

ALTER TABLE {{ binding.schema }}.merge_record
  ADD CONSTRAINT {{ pin.constraint }}
  FOREIGN KEY ({{ s.id_column }}, gate_epoch)
  REFERENCES {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} ({{ s.id_column }}, {{ s.epoch_column }})
  ON UPDATE RESTRICT ON DELETE RESTRICT;
{% endfor %}
